The '''Burawa national football team''' ({{w|Burawi language|Burawi}}: ''Xerka Qaranka ee Burawi'') represents [[Burawa]] in men's international association football. It is governed by the [[Burawi Fotbal Federation]] ({{w|Burawi language|Burawi}}: ''Xisbiga Fotbal ee Burawi'', '''BFF'''), which oversees all fotbal activities in the country. The team is a member of the [[Adonian Football Association]] (AFA) and the [[Mesogean Football Confederation]] (MFC), the Mesogean regional confederation.

Nicknamed '''Xeradaag''' ({{w|Burawi language|Burawi}}: "The Cooperative"), the team is known for its physically demanding, highly coordinated playing style rooted in the country's nationalised academy system, in which most players have grown up playing alongside one another from adolescence. Burawa are one of only ten nations to have won the [[AFA World Cup]], having triumphed at the [[2010 AFA World Cup|2010 tournament]] in [[Nusaraya]]. They have also finished as runners-up twice, at the [[1974 AFA World Cup|1974]] and [[1986 AFA World Cup|1986]] tournaments. Burawa have qualified for every AFA World Cup since their debut in 1958.

== History ==

=== Early years (1956–1970) ===

The Burawa national football team played its first international match on 12 March 1956, a 3–1 victory over [[Galwa]] in [[Kankadadka]]. The match was organised following the formation of the [[Burawi Fotbal Federation]] in 1955, which joined the [[Adonian Football Association]] (AFA) the same year.

Burawa qualified for the inaugural [[1958 AFA World Cup|1958 AFA World Cup]] in [[Volisania]], finishing eighth. The early national team was drawn primarily from the domestic [[Akhyarta League]], with a small number of players from clubs in [[Volisania]] and [[Balisca]]. The team's performance in the 1958 tournament, while modest, established Burawa as a competitive presence in Mesogean football.

The [[1962 AFA World Cup|1962 World Cup]] in [[Zong]] marked Burawa's first major tournament success, with the team finishing third. This was followed by fourth-place finishes at the [[1966 AFA World Cup|1966]] and [[1970 AFA World Cup|1970]] tournaments, establishing Burawa as one of the most consistent teams in the AFA during this period. The team's style during this era was characterised by physical intensity and defensive organisation, traits that would become hallmarks of Burawi football.

=== Rise to prominence and 1974 runners-up ===

By the early 1970s, the domestic league system was producing players of increasing quality, though the best Burawi players were beginning to leave for foreign leagues. The national team, drawn primarily from the domestic [[Akhyarta League]] and a growing diaspora of Burawi players in [[Volisania]], [[Balisca]], and [[Asikyira]], had become a regular presence at the World Cup.

The [[1974 AFA World Cup|1974 World Cup]] in [[Jarin]] produced Burawa's best result to that point. The team advanced through the knockout rounds to reach the final, where they were defeated. The runners-up finish was widely celebrated in Burawa and is credited with raising the profile of the domestic fotbal system, contributing to the political momentum behind the [[1972 Fotbal Reform Act]] that had been signed into law two years earlier.

=== 1972 Fotbal Reform Act and transformation ===

{{Main|1972 Burawi Fotbal Reform Act}}

The [[1972 Fotbal Reform Act]] fundamentally reshaped the Burawi national team by restructuring the domestic league system, creating a nationalised academy programme, and introducing salary caps and a drafting system. The academy system, which began with the [[Samara General Fotbal Academy]] in 1972 and expanded to 16 academies over the following decades, produced generations of players who had trained together from adolescence, creating the deep tactical familiarity and cohesive team movement that would define the national team's style.

However, the reform also had unintended consequences for the national team. The salary cap and the foreign player ban (which lasted until 2002) meant that the domestic league could not retain its best players, who left for wealthier foreign leagues. The national team thus became a rare opportunity for Burawa's best players to play together, and the squad often featured players who had grown up in the same academy cohorts but now played professionally in different countries.

=== 1982 failure and 1986 home World Cup ===

Burawa qualified for the [[1982 AFA World Cup|1982 World Cup]] but failed to advance past the group stage, the only time the team has been eliminated at the first hurdle since their debut in 1958. The early exit was attributed to the disruption of the domestic league following the Fotbal Reform Act, as clubs adjusted to the new drafting system and salary cap structure. The period between 1978 and 1984 is widely regarded as the lowest point in the history of the Burawi national team.

The [[1986 AFA World Cup|1986 World Cup]], hosted by Burawa, marked a dramatic turnaround. Playing on home soil, Burawa advanced through the group stage and knockout rounds to reach the final at [[Iskhal Central Stadium]], where they were defeated 2–1 by [[Asikyira]]. Despite the loss, the tournament was celebrated as a national triumph — the team's cohesive, physically intense style had captivated home audiences, and the hosting was credited with revitalising domestic interest in the sport.

=== Consistent contenders (1990–2006) ===

From 1990 onward, Burawa established themselves as one of the most consistent teams in the AFA, never finishing below sixth at the World Cup for the next two decades. The team finished third at the [[1990 AFA World Cup|1990]], fifth at the [[1994 AFA World Cup|1994]], sixth at the [[1998 AFA World Cup|1998]], and third at both the [[2002 AFA World Cup|2002]] and [[2006 AFA World Cup|2006]] tournaments.

This period was defined by the emergence of the "academy generation" — players who had come through the reformed academy system and who brought an unprecedented level of tactical coordination to the national team. However, the team's inability to convert consistent semi-final appearances into a final victory became a source of frustration, with critics noting that the physically demanding style that made Burawa formidable in the group and early knockout stages left the team vulnerable in the later rounds against more technically fluid opponents.

=== 2010 World Cup champions ===

{{Main|2010 AFA World Cup}}

The [[2010 AFA World Cup|2010 World Cup]] in [[Nusaraya]] marked the pinnacle of Burawi football. Burawa advanced through the group stage and knockout rounds with a series of dominant performances, reaching the final where they won the tournament for the first time, becoming the ninth nation to win the AFA World Cup.

The victory was the culmination of decades of investment in the academy system and the cooperative ownership model. The 2010 squad featured players who had grown up together in the same academy cohorts, many of whom had spent years playing abroad before returning to the national team with the experience and maturity to complement their deep tactical familiarity. The triumph triggered scenes of unprecedented celebration across Burawa, with hundreds of thousands taking to the streets of [[Kankadadka]], [[Iskhal]], and [[Mazar]].

=== Post-championship years (2014–present) ===

Burawa have continued to qualify for every World Cup since 2010, though they have not replicated the success of the Nusaraya tournament. The team finished fifth at the [[2014 AFA World Cup|2014]], third at the [[2018 AFA World Cup|2018]], and fourth at the [[2022 AFA World Cup|2022]].

The current generation continues to be defined by the tension between the academy system's cohesive style and the exodus of top talent to foreign leagues. The 2002 liberalisation of foreign player rules has allowed some experienced international players to join the domestic league, but most of Burawa's best players still play abroad, and the national team remains the primary venue where the country's top talent plays together.

== Stadium ==

Burawa play their home matches at [[Iskhal Central Stadium]] in [[Iskhal]], the country's largest city. The stadium has a capacity of 80,000 and is the primary football venue in the country. Important matches are also occasionally played at [[Mazar National Stadium]] in [[Mazar]] and [[People's Stadium of Iskhal]].

== Playing style ==

The Burawi national team is known for a distinctive playing style shaped by the country's nationalised academy system. Because most of Burawa's top players have trained together from adolescence through the 16-academy system and the [[Burawi Youth League]], the national team exhibits an unusually high degree of tactical familiarity and coordinated team movement. This style is characterised by:

* Intense physical pressing and high-tempo play
* Deep tactical coordination, with players anticipating each other's movements instinctively
* A structured, disciplined approach to both attack and defence
* Emphasis on collective effort over individual brilliance

The style is widely regarded as notably rough and coordinated compared to foreign leagues, and it has proven highly effective in tournament football, where the ability to execute complex tactical systems under pressure is paramount. However, critics note that the approach can appear overly structured and physically draining relative to the more individualistic and technically fluid styles prevalent in other national teams, and that the team has sometimes struggled against opponents who can disrupt the coordinated rhythm.

The nickname '''Xeradaag''' ({{w|Burawi language|Burawi}}: "The Cooperative") reflects both the team's playing style and the cooperative ownership model of the domestic league system, in which players and staff hold a majority stake in their clubs.

